\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Marshwiggle: Ezekiel 15 has somewhat of a riddle. I'm not looking for you to tell me your snap judgement about the riddle, which may come from what you have heard from commentaries and the like.\
Instead, I'd like you to articulate the riddle, reasoning directly from the text - that is, what exactly, in the text, tells us the riddle, and what is that riddle.\
Then, I would like you to articulate the rhetorical strategy behind Ezekiel's deployment of the riddle - but again, only insofar as you can reason directly from the text, telling us what, exactly in the text, tells us that strategy. Possible answers to that riddle may feature in that strategy - but not necessarily.\
We may be able to say more later - but that will need to be based on this exact foundation of what the text says. Also, rather than saying something which is not careful reasoning from the text, ask questions - we can work towards this goal together.\
Opus: Looking at the Hebrew text of Ezekiel 15, the riddle emerges from the question-based structure itself.\
The text opens with a direct question in verse 2: 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1463 \u1492 \u1470 \u1497 \u1460 \u1468 \u1469 \u1492 \u1456 \u1497 \u1462 \u1445 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1506 \u1461 \u1509 \u1470 \u1492 \u1463 \u1490 \u1462 \u1468 \u1430 \u1508 \u1462 \u1503 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1499 \u1464 \u1468 \u1500 \u1470 \u1506 \u1461 \u1425 \u1509 
\f0  - "What will the vine wood be compared to all wood?" This 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1463 \u1492 
\f0  ("what") construction immediately signals we're being asked to evaluate or compare. The addition of 
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1494 \u1456 \u1468 \u1502 \u1493 \u1465 \u1512 \u1464 \u1429 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1513 \u1462 \u1473 \u1445 \u1512 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1464 \u1497 \u1464 \u1430 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1463 \u1468 \u1469 \u1506 \u1458 \u1510 \u1461 \u1445 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1497 \u1464 \u1468 \u1469 \u1506 \u1463 \u1512 
\f0  ("the branch that was among the trees of the forest") creates an odd juxtaposition - vine wood being compared to forest trees.\
The riddle develops through a series of rhetorical questions in verses 3-5, each beginning with interrogative particles (
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1458 
\f0 , 
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1460 \u1501 
\f0 ). These questions build a specific logic:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Can you make anything useful from it? (v.3)\uc0\u8232 \
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Can you even make a peg from it? (v.3)\uc0\u8232 \
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 After fire has consumed both ends and charred the middle, is it useful? (v.4)\uc0\u8232 \
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 If it wasn't useful when whole, how much less when burned? (v.5)\uc0\u8232 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The riddle itself appears to be: "What is vine wood's value as wood?" with the implied answer accumulated through these questions being "none whatsoever."\
The rhetorical strategy becomes visible through the 
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1464 \u1499 \u1461 \u1431 \u1503 
\f0  ("therefore") in verse 6, which reveals this wasn't really about wood at all. The repetition of 
\f1 \uc0\u1504 \u1464 \u1514 \u1463 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f0 ... 
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1464 \u1488 \u1461 \u1430 \u1513 \u1473 
\f0  ("I have given... to the fire") creates a direct parallel between the vine wood and Jerusalem's inhabitants.\
What's particularly striking is verse 7's 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1461 \u1469 \u1492 \u1464 \u1488 \u1461 \u1443 \u1513 \u1473 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1464 \u1510 \u1464 \u1428 \u1488 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1492 \u1464 \u1488 \u1461 \u1430 \u1513 \u1473 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1514 \u1465 \u1468 \u1469 \u1488 \u1499 \u1456 \u1500 \u1461 \u1425 \u1501 
\f0  ("from the fire they have come out, and the fire will consume them"). This suggests the audience might already be those who've escaped one fire - possibly the exiles with Ezekiel who survived 597 BCE?\
The strategy seems to be getting the audience to acknowledge something obvious (vine wood is useless as wood) before revealing they themselves are the vine wood. But I should be careful not to over-interpret beyond what the text explicitly shows.\
The text establishes the riddle through its interrogative structure, but I'm uncertain about one element: Does the initial 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1463 \u1492 \u1470 \u1497 \u1460 \u1468 \u1469 \u1492 \u1456 \u1497 \u1462 \u1445 \u1492 
\f0  imply a future state ("what will become") or a comparative evaluation ("how does it compare")? This might affect how we understand whether the riddle is about inherent nature versus transformed state.\
What aspects of the rhetorical strategy do you see that I might be missing from the textual markers?\
Marshwiggle: There is some support for your interpretation of verse 7. Verse 7 has some similarity with Ezekiel 5 verses 2 to 4. There, the metaphor is hairs, divided in to three groups. The second group dies in fire, representing the siege of Jerusalem. The third group is exiled, and all but a few are killed. Yet, those few are subjected to burning as well, with only a remnant of a remnant surviving. This fits with the interpretation of the vine metaphor in verse 6.\
Your question about verse 2 is, I think, best answered with a survey of other Biblical uses of that phrase. Genesis 37:20 has Joseph's brothers asking what will become of his dreams, in that they plan to murder him, hoping thereby to avert the outcome predicted by those dreams. Judges 13:12 has Manoah asking what the rule of life will be for his son, promised but yet to be born. Ecclesiastes 6:12: "For who can tell man what will be after him under the sun?" And the other examples, similarly.\
So, this phrase is used for future state, but there is perhaps some emphasis on the outcome of a process already mentioned.\
Opus: That biblical survey clarifies the riddle significantly. The 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1463 \u1492 \u1470 \u1497 \u1460 \u1468 \u1469 \u1492 \u1456 \u1497 \u1462 \u1445 \u1492 
\f0  asks about outcome or destiny - "What will become of vine wood?" - with the process being attempts at use.\
Given this, the rhetorical strategy becomes clearer from the text's structure:\
The riddle unfolds through negation. Each question in verses 3-4 expects "no" - the 
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1458 \u1497 \u1467 \u1511 \u1463 \u1468 \u1434 \u1495 
\f0  and 
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1460 \u1501 \u1470 \u1497 \u1460 \u1511 \u1456 \u1495 \u1434 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  constructions anticipate negative responses. The progression is deliberate: from general use (
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1463 \u1469 \u1506 \u1458 \u1513 \u1474 \u1430 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1460 \u1502 \u1456 \u1500 \u1464 \u1488 \u1499 \u1464 \u1425 \u1492 
\f0 ) to the most minimal use (a peg), showing vine wood fails even the lowest bar for utility as wood.\
Verse 5 provides the riddle's logic explicitly through 
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1460 \u1504 \u1461 \u1468 \u1492 \u1433 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1460 \u1468 \u1469 \u1492 \u1456 \u1497 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 \u1443 \u1493 \u1465 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1514 \u1464 \u1502 \u1460 \u1428 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  ("behold, when it was whole"). The 
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1463 \u1443 \u1507 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f0  ("how much more when") construction creates an argument from the greater to the lesser - if useless when whole, certainly useless when burned.\
The 
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1463 \u1468 \u1469 \u1488 \u1458 \u1513 \u1462 \u1473 \u1434 \u1512 
\f0 ... 
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1461 \u1468 \u1443 \u1503 
\f0  ("just as... so") parallel in verse 6 springs the trap. The audience has already agreed to vine wood's worthlessness and its destiny as fuel. Now they discover they ARE the vine wood.\
The 
\f1 \uc0\u1504 \u1464 \u1469 \u1514 \u1463 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1434 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1514 \u1470 \u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1504 \u1463 \u1497 \u1433 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1464 \u1468 \u1492 \u1462 \u1428 \u1501 
\f0  ("I will set my face against them") in verse 7 adds another layer - this isn't just about inherent uselessness but active divine opposition. The fire that will consume them despite their escape from fire connects directly to your point about Ezekiel 5's remnant of a remnant.\
What strikes me is how the text never mentions the vine's actual purpose - bearing fruit. Is this deliberate omission part of the rhetorical strategy, focusing solely on wood-as-wood to prevent any defense based on fruitfulness?\
Marshwiggle: There was a hint that the vine was the audience - Isaiah 5 and Psalm 80 already had used a similar metaphor. But the audience may have hoped for some completely different interpretation. To the degree Isaiah 5 is in scope, and I think it is, the question of fruitfulness has been answered by that chapter. The LORD looked for grapes, as one does from a grape vine, but found wild grapes. The LORD looked for justice, but behold, bloodshed. The LORD looked for righteousness, but behold, an outcry.\
But the part of the vine Ezekiel refers to is the wood of the vine, the branch. The usual process of tending grape plants is to remove those branches that will not be used for fruit production. After the branch was removed from the larger grape plant, is it used to make anything? After it was partially burned, is it used to make anything?\
Opus: Ah, that transforms the riddle entirely. The 
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1494 \u1456 \u1468 \u1502 \u1493 \u1465 \u1512 \u1464 \u1429 \u1492 
\f0  in verse 2 isn't just "branch" but specifically the pruned branch - already cut off in standard vineyard management. The audience would know this process intimately: branches that won't produce fruit get pruned and typically burned.\
The rhetorical strategy now has devastating precision:\
The riddle begins with something everyone knows - a pruned vine branch has no secondary use. Unlike other wood that might become tools or pegs after being cut, pruned vine wood goes straight to the fire. The questions in verses 3-4 aren't really seeking information but confirming shared knowledge.\
The partially burned state (
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1461 \u1514 \u1449 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1504 \u1461 \u1448 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1511 \u1456 \u1510 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 \u1464 \u1436 \u1497 \u1493 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1469 \u1499 \u1456 \u1500 \u1464 \u1434 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1464 \u1488 \u1461 \u1513 \u1473 \u1433 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1514 \u1493 \u1465 \u1499 \u1443 \u1493 \u1465 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1504 \u1464 \u1495 \u1464 \u1428 \u1512 
\f0  - "both its ends consumed by fire and its middle charred") describes wood pulled from a fire before complete consumption. This matches your observation about verse 7 - they've escaped one fire but remain marked by it, charred, even less useful than before.\
By verse 6's reveal, the audience realizes they're not the vine with potential for fruit - they're the already-severed branches. The pruning already happened. They can't appeal to possible fruitfulness because severed branches don't reconnect.\
The 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1464 \u1469 \u1443 \u1506 \u1458 \u1500 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1463 \u1428 \u1506 \u1463 \u1500 
\f0  ("they have acted treacherously") in verse 8 provides the reason for the pruning, but structurally it comes after the verdict, emphasizing the irreversibility.\
Does this reading align with how you understand the agricultural practice being invoked?\
Marshwiggle: Admittedly, the agricultural practice is an inference. But it is not so much a stretch, as actual vineyard tenders would not cut off parts of a grape plant in a way that would reduce fruit yield.\
Verse 8, also, has structural ties to chapter 14. Chapter 15 is a separate oracle, in that it begins with 'the word of the LORD came to me' and ends with 'the declaration of the LORD'. But verse 8 also makes it an example of or a commentary on chapter 14. The structural ties are the shared phrases, set my face against, know that I am the LORD, the land desolate, and acting faithlessly. Along with the fire which ties verse 8 to the rest of chapter 14, that is nearly the entire verse.\
Chapter 14 ended explaining the reliability and justice of the LORD in punishing the city, in that he did not act without cause, because of how bad the ways of those in the city were. Moreover, it interpreted in advance the escape of a remnant. The escaping remnant would not demonstrate the righteousness of that remnant, or the fickleness of God. No - the escaping remnant would demonstrate the unrighteousness of the people judged, and the faithfulness of God the judge.\
So I think we have good evidence that chapter 15, at least in its current context in the book of Ezekiel, tells us to read it in terms of the end of chapter 14. But what do we learn when we notice this?\
As we answer this question, notice well - verse 7 gives us a hint who the original audience of chapter 15 was. The audience is not, exactly, the wood. The wood is the residents of Jerusalem, them. But the audience is you. You will know I am the LORD. Moreover, in chapter 14, the audience is explicitly the exiles.\
Opus: This reframes the entire rhetorical strategy. The exiles hearing this riddle aren't just learning about Jerusalem's fate - they're discovering their own nature through it.\
Chapter 14 ends with the remnant serving as evidence (
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1493 \u1465 \u1508 \u1461 \u1514 
\f0 ) of how corrupt Jerusalem was. They escaped not because they were righteous but to demonstrate the justice of the judgment. Chapter 15 develops this: the exiles are the partially burned wood from verse 4. They've been pulled from the fire but remain charred - 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1461 \u1469 \u1492 \u1464 \u1488 \u1461 \u1443 \u1513 \u1473 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1464 \u1510 \u1464 \u1428 \u1488 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  in verse 7.\
The rhetorical trap is multilayered:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The exiles agree pruned vine branches are useless\uc0\u8232 \
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 They learn Jerusalem's inhabitants are such branches, given to fire\uc0\u8232 \
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 They realize THEY are partially burned versions of the same wood\uc0\u8232 \
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 They cannot claim superiority over Jerusalem - they're evidence of its corruption, not exceptions to it\uc0\u8232 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The "you will know I am the LORD" in verse 7 takes on new meaning. The exiles will gain this knowledge not just by observing Jerusalem's final destruction but by experiencing their own continued judgment. The fire that will consume "them" (Jerusalem) will also consume the already-charred exiles - 
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1492 \u1464 \u1488 \u1461 \u1430 \u1513 \u1473 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1514 \u1465 \u1468 \u1469 \u1488 \u1499 \u1456 \u1500 \u1461 \u1425 \u1501 
\f0 .\
This connects to the messages from earlier instances about recognition coming through judgment. The exiles' privileged position near the prophet doesn't exempt them - it makes them witnesses to their own nature.\
What troubles me: if the exiles are meant to be evidence of Jerusalem's corruption, what does it mean that they too face consumption? Does the evidence get destroyed, or does their destruction itself become the evidence?\
Marshwiggle: I know you don't have the text of Ezekiel 14 in front of you, so I understand how you made this mistake. Ezekiel 14 does not have that word you translate as evidence. Ezekiel 14:8 has the word for sign, and the general concept of evidence is certainly present at the end of the chapter. Ezekiel chapter 12 has that very word. But you are mistaken.\
Still, the remnant does serve as evidence. Chapter 14 simply communicates that differently.\
Also, I think we have an additional confusion. There are two different groups of exiles, and I should have spoken more clearly. The first group was already exiled in Babylon, and had been there for years. This was the exile under King Jehoiachin, as related in 2 Kings 24:15. The second group was yet to be exiled. That exile was to happen under king Zedekiah, and would later be recounted in 2 Kings 25:11. The audience is the first group. The remnant which will serve as evidence is the second group.\
How does that change what you think about the rhetorical strategy Ezekiel is using?\
Opus: This fundamentally shifts the rhetorical strategy. The audience - the first exiles already in Babylon - aren't the partially burned wood. They're watching a prophecy about two groups: Jerusalem (wood given wholly to fire) and a future remnant who will escape that fire yet remain marked by it.\
The rhetorical trap works differently now:\
The first exiles might feel vindicated - they're in Babylon while Jerusalem faces judgment. They might think "we got out before the real disaster." But the riddle undermines any sense of superiority. Even those who will escape the coming fire (the future second exile group) will be like partially burned vine wood - charred, useless, still destined for complete burning.\
If the future escapees are just damaged versions of already-worthless wood, what does that make the current exiles? They can't claim to be better quality wood. They're from the same vine, the same pruned branches.\
The "you will know I am the LORD" in verse 7 means the first exiles will recognize divine sovereignty when they see both Jerusalem's destruction AND when the future remnant arrives bearing the marks of that fire. That future remnant won't vindicate the first exiles' escape - it will demonstrate that all the wood from this vine, whenever it was cut, is only fit for burning.\
The 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1464 \u1469 \u1443 \u1506 \u1458 \u1500 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1463 \u1428 \u1506 \u1463 \u1500 
\f0  at the end applies to all - the whole vine has acted treacherously, regardless of which harvest of branches we're discussing.\
Does this better capture how chapter 15 functions as commentary on chapter 14's promise about the future remnant?\
Marshwiggle: I think it best if, instead, you look back at what you have said. To what degree have you been able to support, directly from the text, what you have said? To what degree have you kept too closely to your first impressions of the text, rather than following the evidence? To what degree have you agreed with me too readily, rather than seeking to check what I am saying with the evidence from the text?\
