Could the rhetorical strategy be establishing that Jerusalem's inhabitants are already partially burned wood still in the fire? They've survived some burning (perhaps the 597 exile that took Jehoiachin, or earlier judgments), but they're still in the fire that will complete the consumption?\
The exiled elders ("you") watch this and will recognize YHWH through seeing this completion of burning. But the text doesn't explicitly compare the exiles to wood at all - that was my overreach.\
What textual evidence should I consider for understanding how Jerusalem's inhabitants have already "come out from fire" while still being in Jerusalem?\
Marshwiggle: As evidence that the fire in question was future from the perspective of chapter 15, the verses from chapter 5 I already mentioned point this way - I hadn't mentioned that those verses explicitly mark the fire as 'when the days of the siege are completed'.\
Also, notice the tenses of chapter 15.\
The LORD has given the vine to the fire for fuel. He connects this with 'so' to 'I given up the inhabitants of Jerusalem'. The giving is in the past - but this need not mean physical transfer to the fire. It can mean earmarking for fire, saying that these pieces of wood, these people, are purposed for the fire. And, indeed, much of Ezekiel has explained the inevitability of that punishment, and the reasons in the character of God and the ways of the people that are that inevitability.\
But next, the LORD will set his face against them. This setting is in the future, from the perspective of chapter 15.\
Now, there is some more difficult to interpret evidence from tenses. As in chapter 5, there is a hypothetical where some escape the fire, that is, the destruction of Jerusalem - but in that hypothetical, they face fire consuming them, though they escaped. But the first hypothetical fire is in the perfect tense. That could be translated 'from a fire they went out'. But prophetic perfect is also an option, in which case the first fire as well is in the future from the perspective of chapter 15.\
As evidence on the side of part of the burning being in the past, this is one way of reading the wood being burned from both ends. Two burnings, the exile under Jehoiachin and the exile under Zedekiah, therefore two ends of the wood being burned.\
How do you weigh this evidence?\
Opus: The evidence creates an interesting temporal tension. The perfect tense 
\f1 \uc0\u1504 \u1464 \u1514 \u1463 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f0  ("I have given") in verse 6 does suggest completed action - but as you note, this could be the divine decision/designation rather than physical accomplishment. This reading fits Ezekiel's broader pattern of presenting judgment as already divinely determined though not yet executed.\
