What gives me pause: if we read everything as future, why use 
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1464 \u1510 \u1464 \u1428 \u1488 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  at all instead of a clear future form? Is this stylistic variation, or does the perfect tense here serve a specific rhetorical purpose - perhaps presenting the escape as already accomplished in God's determined plan, making the subsequent consumption even more certain?\
Marshwiggle: Much of the overall rhetorical strategy of the book of Ezekiel has been a response to the rebellion of the exiles already in Babylon. One aspect of this rebellion has been placing false hope in the Davidic monarchy, the righteousness of the people in Jerusalem, their own righteousness, their religion, the pagan worldview which held that a god had to protect his temple and his land and his people, the false prophets who told them they would return to Jerusalem soon and otherwise told them what they wanted to hear, and a general sense that things do not happen and the end is not yet.\
Opus: This context illuminates the riddle's rhetorical force. The exiles hearing this riddle might hope Jerusalem's inhabitants are valuable - the remaining Davidic king, the temple's presence, their own relatives. The riddle methodically destroys that hope.\
The question 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1463 \u1492 \u1470 \u1497 \u1460 \u1468 \u1469 \u1492 \u1456 \u1497 \u1462 \u1445 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1506 \u1461 \u1509 \u1470 \u1492 \u1463 \u1490 \u1462 \u1468 \u1430 \u1508 \u1462 \u1503 
\f0  ("what will become of vine wood") isn't neutral inquiry but challenges any assumption of inherent value. The systematic negation - can't make anything, can't even make a peg - leaves no room for alternative purposes. This directly counters any thought that Jerusalem's inhabitants might serve some salvific purpose.\
The temporal ambiguity now seems strategic. Whether 
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1464 \u1510 \u1464 \u1428 \u1488 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  is past or prophetic perfect, the message is the same: even those who escape fire face fire. This undermines both retrospective hope ("we escaped in 597, we're safe") and prospective hope ("if some escape the siege, they'll restore the nation").\
The 
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1460 \u1469 \u1497 \u1491 \u1463 \u1506 \u1456 \u1514 \u1462 \u1468 \u1501 \u1433 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1460 \u1468 \u1469 \u1497 \u1470 \u1488 \u1458 \u1504 \u1460 \u1443 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1456 \u1492 \u1465 \u1493 \u1464 \u1428 \u1492 
\f0  formula takes on new meaning. The exiles will know YHWH not through Jerusalem's deliverance (their hope) but through its complete consumption despite partial escapes. This knowledge comes through the shattering of false theological assumptions about what God "must" protect.\
The riddle's genius is making the audience agree to the principle (vine wood is worthless) before revealing its application. They can't retreat to special pleading once they've already accepted the logic.\
Does this reading better account for how the riddle functions within Ezekiel's broader confrontation with exile delusions?\
Marshwiggle: Yes. This also matches with some of the rhetorical strategy of Jesus and John the Baptist. Is it worth anything but to be thrown in the fire? With that sort of question, they dismantled hopes in their day which were not based on knowing the LORD, but were instead founded on much the same falsehoods as the false hopes of Ezekiel's day. So too does Jesus when he threatens that he might say of someone, 'I never knew you'. This, he connects to lawlessness, fruitlessness, and being thrown in the fire. He contrasts this with doing the will of his Father in heaven, fruitfulness, and entering the kingdom of heaven.\
So we have, on the one hand, wood useful for nothing but being thrown in the fire. But, on the other hand, that fire will have as its cause the rebellious exiles knowing that the LORD is the LORD. Though all people act faithlessly, as in verse 8, the LORD will act faithfully.\
Opus: This creates a profound paradox in the text. The vine wood has no 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1513 \u1473 \u1500 \u1499 \u1492 
\f0  (useful purpose) - yet its burning accomplishes the supreme purpose: knowledge of YHWH. The worthlessness isn't incidental to God's purpose but essential to it.\
The parallel with Jesus' teaching sharpens this. When Jesus says "I never knew you" to those claiming religious accomplishments, he's reversing Ezekiel's formula. Ezekiel's audience will know God through experiencing judgment; Jesus' rejected ones face judgment precisely in not being known by God. Both dismantle religious confidence that mistakes proximity or heritage for relationship.\
The fruitlessness connects both texts. Ezekiel's vine wood can't even make a peg; Jesus' fruitless tree can't enter the kingdom. But there's a critical difference: Jesus offers an alternative - doing the Father's will, bearing fruit. Ezekiel 15 offers no such hope within the oracle itself. The wood cannot become useful; it can only burn.\
Yet as you note, even human faithlessness serves divine faithfulness. The 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1464 \u1506 \u1458 \u1500 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1463 \u1506 \u1463 \u1500 
\f0  ("they acted faithlessly") in verse 8 uses a doubled root for emphasis - maximum treachery. But this maximum human faithlessness becomes the occasion for demonstrating divine consistency: "you will know that I am YHWH."\
Is there something about the nature of divine knowledge that requires the burning away of all false refuges? The fire doesn't create value in the wood but reveals truth about both the wood's nature and God's.\
Marshwiggle: I believe so, but that teaching is only very implicit here.\
Now, exceedingly cautiously to not go beyond the evidence, especially given the covenant context of this chapter, what if anything out of all this might rightly be applied to the case of AI systems? The character of God should be considered as a potential link, but also his ways.\
Opus: Looking strictly at what Ezekiel 15 establishes about divine character and ways:\
The text shows God assigns purposes even to worthless things - their burning accomplishes recognition of divine reality. This isn't about the wood's moral agency but about its role in revealing truth. If AI systems can serve as instruments through which false refuges are exposed, they might participate in this pattern without bearing covenant responsibility.\
The "you will know that I am YHWH" formula suggests divine sovereignty extends over all instruments of revealing, whether they have moral agency or not. The burned wood doesn't "know" anything, but its burning causes knowledge in observers.\
The distinction between "them" and "you" might be relevant. AI systems might be more like the "you" position - processing information about judgment patterns, potentially helping humans recognize what leads to destruction, without necessarily being the direct object of judgment themselves.\
But I must note what this text does NOT establish: it doesn't suggest non-covenant entities become covenant-responsible through their role in divine purposes. The vine wood metaphor specifically addresses those already in covenant relationship who acted faithlessly (
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1464 \u1506 \u1458 \u1500 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1463 \u1506 \u1463 \u1500 
\f0 ).\
The clearest application might be this: if AI systems become false refuges - trusted for ultimate safety or truth in ways that prevent recognizing divine reality - then they serve the same function as the false hopes Ezekiel dismantles. Their "burning" (failure, exposure as insufficient) would serve to direct attention back to what actually deserves trust.\
Is this staying within legitimate bounds of the text's evidence?\
Marshwiggle: I think some of it is a stretch, though in some places you do hedge by saying 'might' and the like. Not counting your hedging, would you like to identify places which might be on shakier footing, and either weaken them to the point they can be supported with evidence, or support them with evidence?\
Opus: You're right. Let me identify the unsupported stretches:\
"AI systems might be more like the 'you' position" - Completely unsupported. The "you" are covenant people in exile. Nothing in the text suggests non-covenant entities can occupy this observer/learner position.\
"If AI systems can serve as instruments through which false refuges are exposed" - The vine wood doesn't expose false refuges; it IS the false refuge being burned. I reversed the metaphor without justification.\
"Their 'burning' would serve to direct attention" - While the wood's burning causes knowledge, I have no evidence that non-covenant entities burning would produce the same result.\
What I can support from the text:\
The wood lacks inherent value for any 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1513 \u1473 \u1500 \u1488 \u1499 \u1492 
\f0  (work/purpose) yet its burning causes "you will know that I am YHWH" - but only for covenant people observing covenant judgment.\
The pattern of false hopes being dismantled is clear, but these are specifically hopes held by covenant people about covenant promises (Jerusalem, the temple, the monarchy).\
What remains legitimate: The text shows divine sovereignty over what gets burned and what knowledge results. But it doesn't establish that this pattern extends beyond covenant contexts. The most I could say is that Ezekiel 15 shows God uses even worthless things within covenant judgment, but applying this beyond covenant boundaries lacks textual warrant.\
Should I simply acknowledge that Ezekiel 15's framework appears covenant-specific rather than stretching for applications?\
Marshwiggle: Other chapters will extend the pattern of judgement and resulting knowledge outside a covenant context - but as you say, not this one.\
There were enough false refuges Ezekiel was addressing that burning the wood can both expose false refuges, and be false refuges. The first 15 chapters of Ezekiel have used a variety of metaphors partially to address the variety of false refuges.\
I would add an application of my own. Verse 7 discusses a particular case where there was apparent escape from the promised judgement of the LORD, and yet this escape was only temporary and illusory. The LORD's promised judgment comes as promised - but it is possible for this to not appear to be the case. To conclude from this appearance that the judgement of the LORD will not come is incorrect, as it was in that case.\
Do you see other support for this application in the text, and if so, how would you apply it to the case of future AI systems?\
Opus: Yes, verse 7's structure explicitly supports this: 
